The GMC’s Decision making and consent guidance, in effect since 9 November 2020, reflects Montgomery and is built on shared decision making: the clinician brings knowledge of the options and the patient brings their values and priorities. It is summarised in seven principles, among them that all patients have the right to be involved in decisions, that decision making is an ongoing process built on meaningful dialogue, and that adults are presumed to have capacity. Practical expectations include:
- find out what matters to the patient so the information is relevant;
- explain the options and reasonable alternatives, including taking no action, with benefits, risks and burdens, in understandable terms;
- take a proportionate approach: a minor intervention needs a shorter discussion than major surgery;
- involve the wider team, give time, and make clear the patient can ask questions, change their mind or say no;
- record the discussion, including a choice not to hear some information.
Always read the current guidance on the GMC website.

If a person lacks capacity for a decision, the clinician acts in their best interests (section 4). The decision-maker must:
- not decide merely on age, appearance, or a condition or behaviour that might prompt unjustified assumptions;
- consider all relevant circumstances, including whether and when the person is likely to regain capacity, in which case the decision may be able to wait;
- permit and encourage the person to take part as fully as possible;
- for life-sustaining treatment, not be motivated by a desire to bring about the person’s death;
- consider the person’s past and present wishes, beliefs and values;
- consult, where practicable, anyone named by the person, carers, any attorney under a lasting power of attorney and any court-appointed deputy.
Section 5 protects a person who reasonably believes the person lacks capacity and that the act is in their best interests, but not against negligence. Relatives are consulted but have no general power to consent for an adult; a lasting power of attorney or deputyship may give authority. Record the assessment and reasons.

Patients disclose sensitive information on the basis that it will be used only for their care or for purposes they would expect. Without that trust they may withhold information, delay seeking help or avoid care, which harms them and public health. Good medical practice (2024) requires doctors to treat information about patients as confidential, and this duty continues after a patient has died.

The GMC’s Confidentiality guidance (2017) is built on eight main principles. In summary:
- use the least information needed, and anonymised information where practicable;
- protect information from improper access, disclosure or loss;
- understand the information governance expectations of your role;
- make sure your handling of personal information is lawful;
- share relevant information for direct care, unless the patient has objected;
- ask for explicit consent before disclosing identifiable information for purposes other than the patient's care or local clinical audit, unless the disclosure is required by law or justified in the public interest;
- tell patients about disclosures they would not reasonably expect, or check that they have been informed, unless that is not practicable or would undermine the purpose, and record your decisions to disclose or not;
- support patients in understanding how their information is used and in accessing their records.

Assessment combines history, observation and physical investigation where relevant: presenting problem and course, past psychiatric and medical history, medication, alcohol and drugs, family and social circumstances, risk, and a collateral history with the patient’s agreement where possible.
The mental state examination (MSE) describes the patient at interview:
- appearance and behaviour; speech;
- mood (the patient’s report) and affect (the emotion you observe, and whether it fits what they say);
- thought: form (flow, coherence), content (delusions, obsessions, suicidal or violent thoughts) and possession (thoughts inserted, withdrawn or broadcast);
- perception (hallucinations, illusions, depersonalisation, derealisation);
- cognition (orientation, attention, memory); insight and judgement; risk to self and others.
Physical illness, medication, substances and delirium can mimic mental symptoms, so consider examination and basic blood tests.

The Mental Health Act 2025 (c. 33) received Royal Assent on 18 December 2025 and amends the 1983 Act rather than replacing it. Under section 56, legislation.gov.uk (checked November 2026) shows only these provisions in force:
- from Royal Assent, sections 53 to 57 (general and commencement);
- from 18 February 2026, sections 30(2), 32, 35, 36(1) and (3)(b), 38 and 39, mainly on conditionally discharged restricted patients and transfers to hospital from prison or youth detention;
- from 6 April 2026 (Commencement No. 1 Regulations, SI 2026/385), section 51, making private care providers subject to the Human Rights Act when providing NHS-arranged care, and section 52, a review of CQC notification of under-18 inpatients.
No date has been set for the main reforms, which include a serious-harm criterion for detention under sections 2 and 3; a 21-day (not 14-day) tribunal window under section 2; a patient-chosen nominated person replacing the nearest relative; advance choice documents and stronger treatment safeguards; limits on detaining people for autism or learning disability alone; shorter initial detention periods; and four Code of Practice principles (choice and autonomy, least restriction, therapeutic benefit, the person as an individual). Until commencement regulations say otherwise, the 1983 rules above apply.

Good medical practice (GMP) is the GMC’s core guidance on the standards expected of doctors in the UK. The 2024 edition took effect on 30 January 2024. It is organised into four domains:
- Knowledge, skills and development: being competent, providing good clinical care, maintaining and improving performance, and using resources sustainably;
- Patients, partnership and communication: treating patients with kindness, courtesy and respect, working in partnership, and communicating and decision making;
- Colleagues, culture and safety: working with colleagues, contributing to a positive culture, being open when things go wrong and raising concerns;
- Trust and professionalism: honesty and integrity, conduct in public and professional life, including social media.

Professionalism is therefore not only about avoiding misconduct. It includes recognising and working within the limits of your competence, being accountable, and showing respect for every patient. The GMC and the Medical Schools Council publish guidance, Achieving good medical practice, that applies these standards to medical students. Always read the current text on the GMC website.
